WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.110
JOHN BOHANNON: This
is Michelle Casbon.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.208
She's an engineer at
Google on the Kubeflow team

00:00:11.208 --> 00:00:13.000
MICHELLE CASBON: And
this is John Bohannon.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.553
He's the director of science
at Primer, an AI startup.

00:00:15.553 --> 00:00:18.220
JOHN BOHANNON: My team at Primer
built a machine learning system

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.740
that reads millions of
news articles and tells you

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.485
what it learns.

00:00:21.485 --> 00:00:23.110
MICHELLE CASBON: And
I worked with them

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:24.910
to deploy this
system of Kubeflow

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.013
to make it portable
and scalable.

00:00:27.013 --> 00:00:28.930
JOHN BOHANNON: The goal
is to create a machine

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.670
to help humans learn
about the world

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.500
and overcome their biases.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.540
Humans are biased.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.158
MICHELLE CASBON:
Consider this riddle.

00:00:36.158 --> 00:00:38.450
JOHN BOHANNON: A boy and his
father are in a car crash.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.185
The father is killed instantly.

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:41.560
The boy is rushed
to the hospital

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.393
and prepped for surgery
with one of the best

00:00:43.393 --> 00:00:45.220
doctors in the country.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.350
The doctor arrives, sees
the boy on the table,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.420
and says, "I can't
operate on this patient.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.600
He's my son."

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.528
MICHELLE CASBON:
Who's the doctor?

00:00:52.528 --> 00:00:54.070
JOHN BOHANNON: It's
a paradox, right?

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.945
Father's dead, so--

00:00:56.945 --> 00:00:58.570
MICHELLE CASBON: It's
the boy's mother.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.945
JOHN BOHANNON: For those
playing at home, don't feel bad.

00:01:00.945 --> 00:01:02.834
85% of people are
stumped by this question.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:04.959
It even worked on Michelle
when she first heard it.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:06.820
MICHELLE CASBON:
Bias is powerful.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.920
All humans have
it, and we all have

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.488
to work against it constantly.

00:01:10.488 --> 00:01:12.280
JOHN BOHANNON: Every
time you meet someone,

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.900
you can't help but
make assumptions.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.780
MICHELLE CASBON: But
you're probably biased.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:17.650
JOHN BOHANNON: Wait.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.090
That one's actually mine.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.710
MICHELLE CASBON:
And that one's mine.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:21.920
Oh.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:23.513
JOHN BOHANNON: You're
totally biased.

00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:25.930
MICHELLE CASBON: Well, any
system built on human decisions

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.270
will inherit human bias.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:29.920
JOHN BOHANNON:
Consider Wikipedia.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.560
It has grown into the world's
most important general purpose

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.500
information resource, but
there's no one in charge.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.330
Information is added
to Wikipedia day

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.893
after day by millions
of volunteers.

00:01:40.893 --> 00:01:43.060
It just boggles the mind
that such a thing can exist

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.280
and that it actually works.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.190
Wikipedia aims to be the
summary of all human knowledge,

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.695
but it has biases, just like
the humans who create it.

00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:54.070
MICHELLE CASBON: If you think
about all the new things we

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.860
learn about the world each day,
keeping Wikipedia up to date

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.115
seems impossible.

00:01:59.115 --> 00:02:01.240
It's not just that important
information is missing

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.570
from most articles, but it's
that many important articles

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.520
haven't been written at all.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.070
JOHN BOHANNON: Which shouldn't
be that surprising, right?

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.028
MICHELLE CASBON: Well--
so there are about five

00:02:11.028 --> 00:02:12.400
million Wikipedia articles.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.227
JOHN BOHANNON: In
English alone, yep.

00:02:14.227 --> 00:02:16.810
MICHELLE CASBON: And there are
some thousands of news articles

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:18.205
published every day.

00:02:18.205 --> 00:02:20.740
JOHN BOHANNON: Hundreds of
thousands, just in English.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.000
MICHELLE CASBON: So you
just have to read all of it.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.000
You find the important
information that's in one

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.210
set and doesn't
exist in the other.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:28.960
OK.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.460
Yeah, that's hard.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.710
JOHN BOHANNON: Machine
learning to the rescue.

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.270
I decided to start by
focusing on people.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.290
We want a system that just
reads all the news, every day,

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.420
and learns new information
about everyone,

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.190
not only people
described in Wikipedia

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.695
but anyone described
in the news.

00:02:44.695 --> 00:02:46.570
And if there's enough
significant information

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:49.120
about someone in the news
who's missing from Wikipedia,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.420
the system should just write you
the first draft of the article.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.010
The goal is to
make it as easy as

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.593
possible for the human editors
of Wikipedia to do their job,

00:02:57.593 --> 00:03:00.010
and the first step is to shine
a really bright light so we

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.862
can see where our bias is.

00:03:01.862 --> 00:03:03.070
MICHELLE CASBON: For example?

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.320
JOHN BOHANNON: Well, for
example, only about 15%

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.350
of Wikipedia's biographical
articles are about women,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.800
and that gender gap is
especially bad for scientists.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.290
If you never hear about all
the amazing women of science

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.020
and engineering, then
it reinforces your bias

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.610
about those professions.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.720
MICHELLE CASBON: Exactly.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.850
And that's why I wanted to
work with Primer on this

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:22.925
in the first place.

00:03:22.925 --> 00:03:23.800
JOHN BOHANNON: Right.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.510
And here we are just
a few weeks later,

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.135
and we're actually
catching up on where

00:03:27.135 --> 00:03:28.450
we are with deployment.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:32.620
So where we left off, we had
this really cool ML system

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.720
with some really classic
ML deployment problems.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.535
MICHELLE CASBON:
Kubeflow to the rescue.

00:03:37.535 --> 00:03:39.410
JOHN BOHANNON: Our first
problem was scaling,

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.070
so the computing load is all
over the map on this one.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.410
On any given day, we're
processing hundreds

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.860
of thousands of documents-- news
articles, scientific papers--

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.380
and we're updating information
about people in our knowledge

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:51.260
base.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.110
But we also have
users who might want

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.700
to add thousands of
new people all at once,

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.240
and that kicks off a
back-fill of information

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.360
about those people from millions
of historical documents.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.943
MICHELLE CASBON:
This turned out to be

00:04:02.943 --> 00:04:05.590
easy to solve because your team
had already set up a Kubernetes

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:06.690
cluster.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:10.120
Kubeflow is built on Kubernetes
is because scalability

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.273
is baked into the platform.

00:04:12.273 --> 00:04:13.690
The application
back-end was built

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:15.700
on top of Postgres, which
was a straightforward

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.560
migration into Cloud SQL.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.320
Once the data was migrated,
I transplanted the full app

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.029
into Kubernetes Engine.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:23.770
I started with a
single node cluster

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.110
and enabled auto
provisioning, which

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.420
scales the size of the
cluster up and down based

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.590
on resource requests.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:32.680
So now we can deal with
unpredictable and bursty

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.090
computing loads like yours.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:34.840
JOHN BOHANNON: OK.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.360
But on top of that
scaling problem,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.720
I've also got a
complexity problem.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.110
So our data pipeline has a
bunch of spaghetti junctions,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.080
and not only do
we have thousands

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.770
of models to load
at inference time,

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.380
but we have to
update those models

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.030
as new information comes in.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.570
So at training, it adds up
to millions of model features

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:53.817
getting updated every day.

00:04:53.817 --> 00:04:55.150
MICHELLE CASBON: I noticed that.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.460
Well, luckily, this is exactly
what Kubeflow is designed for--

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.250
composability.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.400
The platform provides patterns
for reusing common ML tasks,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.200
like updating
models incrementally

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.070
in the background.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.150
I started by carving
out pieces of the app.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.100
For example, model
training was refactored

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.620
into a separate service that
uses Kubeflow operators.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:19.380
The monolith is now separated
into smaller, distinct pieces.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.890
Now it's a pipeline with three
separate steps, train, serve,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.240
and web app.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.990
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh,
that sounds much nicer.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:26.490
OK.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.767
So how's it looking so far?

00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:29.850
MICHELLE CASBON: Well, the
refactoring immediately

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.340
reduced the training runtime,
so that translates directly

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.320
to cost savings.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.140
And now you don't have to
run huge nodes all the time

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.110
because GKE takes care
of the scaling for you.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.290
Well, the other win is the
decrease in code complexity.

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:46.860
That translates to less
maintenance overhead,

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.040
less time searching for the
source of bugs, less time

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.087
on ramping new engineers,
and adding new features.

00:05:53.087 --> 00:05:55.170
JOHN BOHANNON: OK, but
that creates a new problem.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:56.490
MICHELLE CASBON: What
haven't I thought of?

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.890
JOHN BOHANNON: So now we
have all this saved time.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.690
What am I going to
do with all my time?

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.250
MICHELLE CASBON: You could
create some Wikipedia articles

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.528
about women of science.

00:06:03.528 --> 00:06:04.320
JOHN BOHANNON: Hmm.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:05.107
Good call.

00:06:05.107 --> 00:06:07.440
Oh, and what about the last
thing on my wish list, about

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.060
vendor lock-in?

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.820
We don't want our system tied
to one cloud infrastructure.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.330
We want it to be able to
deploy anywhere, even on prem.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.250
Oh, and bonus points
if the solution

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.450
is free and open source.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:20.910
MICHELLE CASBON:
Check and check.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.868
As long as you're running
standard, open source

00:06:22.868 --> 00:06:24.345
Kubernetes, you
can run Kubeflow.

00:06:24.345 --> 00:06:26.220
JOHN BOHANNON: Wait,
Kubeflow is open source?

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:26.720
What?

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.870
MICHELLE CASBON: Yep.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.350
Thanks for listening.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:30.990
I hope that hearing
about this project

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.300
lowers the bar for
trying it yourself.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.370
The Kubeflow community
has some great resources

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:38.440
for getting started, which
you can find at Kubeflow.org.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.920
JOHN BOHANNON: And, hey, got
a few minutes of free time?

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.837
I've already used this
machine learning system

00:06:42.837 --> 00:06:44.880
to identify thousands of
notable women of science

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.130
who are missing from Wikipedia.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.630
You can make a
difference right now.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.180
Just follow this link
and see what the machine

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.710
has discovered for you.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.630
Go fill in a gap in
the world's knowledge.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:58.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

